.Dd July 20, 2026
.Dt SERVICED 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm serviced
.Nd service manifest format for 5BSD
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Service manifests are UCL files that declare one service each.
They are typically embedded in capability bundles under
.Pa etc/
and parsed by
.Xr libcapbundle 3 .
A bundle may contain at most 32 service manifests, and at most 64 services may
be loaded across the complete bundle set.
.Pp
Labels must be globally unique across all manifests.
Bundle identifiers must be globally unique across the loaded bundle set.
Labels and capability names should use reverse-domain format
(containing at least one dot) for the naming registry.
.Ss Required Keys
.Bl -tag -width "program"
.It Va bundle_id
Stable reverse-domain identifier shared by every service manifest in the
bundle.
.It Va program
Path relative to the bundle's
.Pa bin/
directory.
Absolute paths, empty paths, and paths containing
.Dq ..
are rejected.
.It Va provides
A globally unique service name or array of names.
The first name is the service label used by serviced.
.El
.Ss Optional Keys
.Bl -tag -width "max_failures"
.It Va version
Bundle version string.
If present, it must match across every service manifest in the bundle.
.It Va author
Bundle author string.
If present, it must match across every service manifest in the bundle.
.It Va arguments
Array of literal argument strings placed after
.Va argv[0] .
No shell is invoked and no word splitting, expansion, or substitution occurs.
Maximum 32 arguments of 255 bytes each.
.It Va environment
Object mapping environment-variable names to string values.
Maximum 32 entries.
Names use the portable identifier form (a letter or underscore followed by
letters, digits, or underscores), values must be strings, and each resulting
.Dq name=value
entry must be shorter than 1024 bytes.
Names beginning with
.Dq ORACLED_
are reserved and rejected.
.It Va user
Drop privileges to this user before executing the service.
The user is resolved via
.Xr getpwnam 3
and supplementary groups via
.Xr getgrouplist 3
at exec time.
If the user does not exist when the service is launched, the
service fails to start and the error is logged.
.It Va group
Drop privileges to this primary group.
Overrides the group obtained from
.Va user
if both are specified.
If the group does not exist at launch time, the service fails to start.
.It Va restart
Restart policy when the service exits.
One of:
.Bl -tag -width "on-failure" -compact
.It Dq never
Do not restart the service.
This is the default.
.It Dq always
Restart unconditionally after exit.
.It Dq on-failure
Restart only on non-zero exit status or signal death.
.El
.It Va stop_timeout
Seconds to wait after sending
.Dv SIGTERM
before escalating to
.Dv SIGKILL .
Must be in the range 1\(en300.
Default: 5.
.It Va max_failures
Circuit breaker threshold.
After this many consecutive rapid failures, the service is permanently
disabled until the next manifest reload.
Must be in the range 1\(en100.
Default: 10.
.It Va requires
A service name or array of service names this service depends on.
Maximum 8 entries.
The service will not start until all required capabilities are
available.
.It Va on_demand
Boolean.
If true, the service is not started during initial launch but instead
launched on first
.Xr service_lookup 3
request for one of its provided names.
Default: false.
.It Va kmod_requires
String or array of up to 8 kernel module names that must be loaded before
launch.
Only system bundles may request module loading.
Names may contain only letters, digits, period, underscore, and hyphen;
paths are rejected.
The request is executed by
.Xr oracled 8
under its nonce so
.Nm
does not retain ambient kernel-module authority.
.It Va capabilities
Object containing capability tokens and service descriptors to delegate.
See
.Sx Capabilities
below.
.El
.Pp
Unknown keys, wrong value types, excess array entries, invalid capability
entries, and out-of-range values reject the entire bundle.
Security declarations are never silently discarded or truncated.
Reload validation is transactional: a rejected replacement leaves the previous
registry and running services intact.
.Ss Capabilities
The
.Va capabilities
object requests capability tokens from
.Xr oracled 8
on behalf of the service.
Resources listed here that are not pre-declared in
.Xr oracled.conf 5
are automatically claimed by the oracle at runtime.
These
.Em dynamic claims
are reference-counted across all services; when the last service
using a dynamic claim exits, the claim is released from the kernel.
Resources that are declared in
.Xr oracled.conf 5
are
.Em policy claims
and are never released.
.Pp
Every requested isolation authority is minted before
.Xr pdfork 2 .
If any mint fails or the set is incomplete, the program is not executed.
The token descriptors are inherited after
.Xr execve 2
and named by
.Ev ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS .
They remain inactive until the program calls
.Fn service_authorize_capabilities
from
.Xr libservice 3
or explicitly performs the equivalent authorization operation.
The standard library operation consumes the activation handles after
validating the complete set, closes the inherited descriptor numbers, and
removes
.Ev ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS
so a later program image cannot discover the inherited descriptor numbers.
It retains private close-on-exec references for the process lifetime because
closing the last reference would revoke the kernel authorization.
Likewise, process shielding is a program decision made through
.Fn service_protect
from
.Xr libservice 3 ;
it is not a manifest or supervisor policy.
.Pp
This object covers every delegable token family implemented by the
mac_capability isolation and system services, plus the explicitly requested
mount, process-node, accounting, and identity service descriptors.
Channel, coalition, procdesc, Capsicum, and capprotect descriptors remain
lifecycle plumbing or explicit per-process confinement decisions.
.Pp
It may contain the following sub-keys:
.Bl -tag -width "network"
.It Va paths
Array of absolute filesystem paths (maximum 16).
.Xr serviced 8
requests an isolation access token from the oracle for each path
and passes it to the service at launch.
.It Va files
Array of fine-grained file capability objects (maximum 16).
Each object contains:
.Bl -tag -width "actions" -compact
.It Va path
Absolute path to the target file.
Required.
.It Va actions
A string, array of strings, or
.Dq * .
Individual action names are:
.Dq read ,
.Dq write ,
.Dq exec ,
.Dq create ,
.Dq delete ,
.Dq stat ,
.Dq lookup ,
.Dq append ,
.Dq rename_from ,
.Dq rename_to ,
.Dq link ,
.Dq setattr ,
.Dq truncate ,
and
.Dq connect .
The special values
.Dq *
and
.Dq all
grant all actions.
.El
.It Va network
Array of network endpoint capability objects (maximum 16).
Each object contains:
.Bl -tag -width "direction" -compact
.It Va domain
Address family:
.Dq inet
(default),
.Dq inet6 ,
.Dq bluetooth ,
or
.Dq * .
.It Va protocol
Transport protocol:
.Dq tcp ,
.Dq udp ,
.Dq hci ,
.Dq l2cap ,
.Dq rfcomm ,
.Dq sco ,
.Dq iso ,
or
.Dq * .
.It Va port
Integer port number, or
.Dq * .
.It Va ports
Port range as a string
.Dq min-max ,
or
.Dq * .
Only one of
.Va port
or
.Va ports
should be specified.
.It Va direction
.Dq bind
(default),
.Dq connect ,
or
.Dq * .
.It Va address
IP address or CIDR notation
.Pq e.g., Dq 10.0.0.0/24 ,
or a Bluetooth BD_ADDR when
.Va domain
is
.Dq bluetooth .
Optional.
A specific address requires an explicit, matching
.Va domain ;
the wildcard domain may only be combined with a wildcard address.
.It Va prefix
CIDR prefix length as an integer.
Optional; provides an alternative to embedding the prefix in
.Va address .
It requires a specific address.
.El
.It Va jails
Array of jail operation tokens (maximum 16).
Grants the service authority to perform jail operations via
delegated capability tokens.
An entry may be a positive numeric JID, a non-empty jail name, or an object
containing
.Va jid
and/or
.Va name
plus optional
.Va actions .
Actions are
.Dq create ,
.Dq get ,
.Dq set ,
.Dq remove ,
and
.Dq attach ;
omitting actions grants all jail actions.
.It Va services
Array containing any of
.Dq mount ,
.Dq node ,
.Dq accounting ,
or
.Dq identity
(maximum 4, without duplicates).
These entries request authority-bearing mac_capability service descriptors,
not activation tokens.
They are delivered through
.Ev ORACLED_CAPABILITY_FDS
and obtained by name with
.Fn service_capability_fd
from
.Xr libservice 3 .
.It Va vsock
Array of VSOCK endpoint capability objects (maximum 16).
Each object accepts
.Va cid
as an integer or
.Dq any ,
.Va port
as a 32-bit integer or
.Va ports
as
.Dq min-max ,
and
.Va direction
as
.Dq bind ,
.Dq connect ,
or
.Dq any .
The defaults are any CID, all ports, and bind direction.
.It Va system
Array of system gate names (maximum 11).
.Xr serviced 8
requests a system access token from the oracle with the specified
gates and passes it to the service.
The complete set is
.Dq kldload ,
.Dq kldunload ,
.Dq kldstat ,
.Dq reboot ,
.Dq swapon ,
.Dq swapoff ,
.Dq sysctl ,
.Dq kenv ,
.Dq acct ,
.Dq audit ,
and
.Dq kenv_read .
.El
.Ss Dependency Resolution
Before launching services,
.Xr serviced 8
topologically sorts all loaded manifests by their
.Va provides
and
.Va requires
declarations.
Services that provide capabilities are started before services
that require them.
.Pp
The following rules apply:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Dq ORACLED
is an implicit provider that is always satisfied.
Services may list it in
.Va requires
without a corresponding
.Va provides .
.It
Unknown providers reject the bundle set.
.It
Dependency cycles are fatal: no services are started if a cycle is
detected.
.It
Duplicate
.Va label
values or duplicate
.Va provides
names across manifests cause the entire reload to be rejected.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Capabilities/System/"
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/
System capability bundles.
Each
.Pa *.cap
directory may contain Service.ucl files in the same format;
see
.Xr libcapbundle 3 .
.It Pa /Capabilities/
User-installed capability bundles.
.El
.Pp
Both bundle roots and every loaded bundle tree must be owned by root and must
not be writable by group or other.
Symlinks and non-regular objects inside a bundle are rejected.
This makes manifests executable security policy rather than mutable
application data.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Minimal service manifest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# myservice.ucl
bundle_id = "org.example.myservice";
program = "myservice";
provides = ["org.example.myservice"];
restart = "always";
.Ed
.Pp
Service with dependencies and file capabilities:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bundle_id = "org.example.webapp";
program = "webapp";
provides = ["org.example.webapp"];
arguments = ["--foreground", "--config", "/etc/webapp.conf"];
environment { APP_MODE = "production"; }
user = "www";
group = "www";
requires = ["database"];
restart = "on-failure";
stop_timeout = 10;
max_failures = 20;
capabilities {
    paths = ["/var/www"];
    files = [
        {
            path = "/var/log/webapp.log";
            actions = ["write", "create", "append"];
        },
    ];
    network = [
        {
            port = 8080;
            protocol = "tcp";
            direction = "bind";
            address = "127.0.0.1";
        },
        {
            domain = "inet";
            protocol = "tcp";
            direction = "connect";
            address = "10.0.0.0/24";
            ports = "5432-5433";
        },
    ];
    system = ["sysctl"];
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libcapbundle 3 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_isolation 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_system 4 ,
.Xr oracled.conf 5 ,
.Xr oracled 8 ,
.Xr servicectl 8 ,
.Xr serviced 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
manifest format first appeared in 5BSD.
